            {
              "_key": "k52",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "k53",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Not every change to a cap table is a funding round. When shareholders approve a pool of 1,00,000 options, no money comes in, but the fully diluted share count rises from 11,00,000 to 12,00,000. Everyone is diluted immediately, even though no option has been granted yet.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ],
              "markDefs": []
            },
            {
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "insight",
              "text": "Creating the pool before the seed round means the founders and angels bear all of its dilution, and the seed fund bears none. Investors often insist on exactly this timing. Lesson 3.3 explains why it matters so much.",
              "_key": "k54"
            },
